Z||000000||tomcat06085||10-13-2001||12:04 PM||||We got an 88 GT with automatic that is warmed up...has started with no problems in the past and now won't kick over. Besides checking for loose connections to the starter motor and checking engine temp...what would be the obvious thing to look for. Haven't experienced this in several test drives.<P>------------------<BR>tomcat<BR>Help me go fast!||||reg||5
Z||000001||tomcat06085||10-13-2001||12:28 PM||||If a starter motor is fine, all connections are good...is there any other problematic symptoms to look for in the electrical system beyond the engine compartment???<P>We want to put this car on a truck to get it from New Jersey to Connecticut, but don't want to have to fix it more than once.<P>Thanks in advance<P>------------------<BR>tomcat<BR>Help me go fast!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fierowrecker||10-13-2001||12:47 PM||||Hey Tomcat!<BR>If you were to do a search on the subject, you will find it is a common problem with the Fiero...<BR>Replace the starter and add a heat shield...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||10-13-2001||03:04 PM||||if not a overheating starter or bad battery connection, could just be ignition timing is advanced too much.||||reg||1
Z||000004||hugh||10-13-2001||05:34 PM||||Toms refering to the 88GT he got from me.I've had the car for about 8 months.I only put about 150 miles on it.I drove the car over to his cousin's moving business(about 3 or 4 miles) and he asked to take it for another test drive,as it was wet when he first drove it.I said fine.He took another drive,came back and said it was ok.We went into his office to complete the paperwork.I was going to pull the car into his shop and the car did not crank.I hit the key while moving the shift lever from park to reverse to neutral and saw a change in the voltage reading,meaning to me that the starter solenoid was energized with the key and I was making and breaking the start solenoid circuit with the shift lever.I figure from this the solenoid has a heatsoak problem and may be fine when it cools off.If that is the case then the best fix is to remove the starter and if it is ok,cut the spring in the solenoid that returns the Bendix gear and solenoid contacts to their normal open.This makes it easier for the solenoid to pull in and the motor will still have plenty of available power to spin the engine.If it's not ok,still do the same to the new starter's solenoid.The voltage never fell below about ten volts.Any other Ideas?  ||||reg||5
Z||000005||tomcat06085||10-13-2001||06:13 PM||||Hi guys...thanks for the tips and clues...like Hugh said, starter solenoid spring is probably doing it...I had a Merc Cougar XR7 with the performance 351 ci and it was fine until the warm weather. In Connecticut it would start all winter like a champ, but when spring came, that V8 with the works on would heat up the starter motor and a hour later it would crank, but most of the time it was drive it, park it and hope you're appointment lasted longer than hour. I'll let you know when my cousin tries it later in the weekend.<P>------------------<BR>tomcat<BR>Help me go fast!||||reg||3
